<v Robyn Semien>But the DOJ recommendation I was most interested in was number 4.2.1. "Las Vegas Police should provide its commanders, supervisors, and officers with advanced, specialized training in fair and impartial policing." In other words, implicit bias training.
<v Robyn Semien>但我最感兴趣的司法部建议是4.2.1。“拉斯维加斯警察应该为指挥官、主管和警官提供高级的、专门的‘公平与公正警务’培训。”换句话说，内隐偏见训练。

00:50:33.580 --> 00:50:33.710
<v Robyn Semien>I was surprised to learn it was one of the last reforms, of the many reforms, the Vegas police tackled. I'd assumed bias training would be one of the first things any department would do. But classes just began in October. Which means they made huge progress --they bought the number of unjust police shootings to zero --months before they asked individual officers to sit down and think about their own biases.
<v Robyn Semien>我很惊讶地发现这是拉斯维加斯警察处理的众多改革中的最后几项改革之一。我以为偏见训练会是任何部门做的第一件事。但课程直到十月才开始。这意味着他们在要求个别警官坐下来思考自己的偏见之前，就已经取得了巨大进展——把不当警察枪击事件降到了零。

00:50:33.710 --> 00:51:03.350
<v Robyn Semien>There were no classes scheduled when we visited. But we got to talk to a few of the instructors about what they teach, the most candid of whom was Training Officer Marla Stevens. She's third-generation police, been with the force for 15 years. She says cops can be a rough crowd, a really rough crowd, especially if they're at a class that's required and that has the word bias in it. So she's strategic about how she kicks things off. She says she starts her class by saying, I understand some of you don't want to be here, and I get that.
<v Robyn Semien>我们去的时候没有安排课程。但我们确实和几位讲师谈了谈他们教的内容，其中最坦诚的是训练警官玛拉·史蒂文斯（Marla Stevens）。她是第三代警察，已经在警队工作了15年。她说警察可能是一群粗鲁的人，非常粗鲁，特别是如果他们参加的课程是强制性的，而且名字里带有“偏见”这个词。所以她在开始时采取了战略。她说她开始上课时会说：我理解你们中有些人不想待在这里，我懂。

00:51:03.350 --> 00:51:25.410
<v Marla Stevens>I talk about how we got to this place. And I tell them, you know, this started with the officer-involved shootings that we had in 2011. The fact is, did we shoot more unarmed black men than we did white? Absolutely, we did. And we put those numbers up there and show them to our officers and say, these are the stats. You can't argue with those numbers.
<v Marla Stevens>我谈论我们是如何走到这一步的。我告诉他们，你知道，这一切始于我们在2011年发生的警察涉案枪击事件。事实是，我们枪杀的无辜黑人男性是否比白人多？当然，确实如此。我们把这些数字放在那里，向我们的警察展示并说，这些就是统计数据。你无法与这些数字争辩。

00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:41.090
<v Robyn Semien>From there, she eases them to the concept of bias, baby steps, first acknowledging biases nobody finds shameful or controversial. She says her class is filled with truck-loving men, so they talk about the thing that divides them-- Chevy or Ford.
<v Robyn Semien>从那里，她引导他们进入偏见的概念，循序渐进，首先承认没人觉得羞耻或有争议的偏见。她说她的班里坐满了爱车男，所以他们谈论分裂他们的事情——雪佛兰还是福特。

00:51:41.090 --> 00:51:44.830
<v Marla Stevens>And start the whole fight in the audience of which truck is better.
