<v Marla Stevens>And if you can't fix it, then maybe you're not in the right line of work. You know, maybe this isn't for you. I'm not afraid to say that to somebody, either.
<v Marla Stevens>如果你不能修正它，那么也许你并不适合这份工作。你知道，也许这不适合你。我也不会害怕对别人说出这一点。

00:52:35.740 --> 00:52:37.230
<v Robyn Semien>Really?
<v Robyn Semien>真的吗？

00:52:37.230 --> 00:52:49.830
<v Marla Stevens>Look, I'm at an age now, I just don't care. I mean, what's right is right, and what's wrong is wrong. And if you want to sit around and say, oh, this is all [BLEEP], well, maybe this isn't the place for you.
<v Marla Stevens>看，我已经到了这个年纪，我不在乎了。我是说，对的就是对的，错的就是错的。如果你想坐在这里说，噢，这全是废话，那么，也许这不适合你。

00:52:49.830 --> 00:52:58.290
<v Brett Brosnahan>The takeaway from the class, what I liked most about when they were teaching us about biases, is having a bias isn't being racist.
<v Brett Brosnahan>从课上学到的东西，关于他们教我们偏见的部分，我最喜欢的是：有偏见并不等于种族主义。

00:52:58.290 --> 00:53:04.920
<v Robyn Semien>This is officer Brett Brosnahan. He's been on the force for six years and took the implicit bias class. He talked with Sean.
<v Robyn Semien>这是布雷特·布罗斯纳汉（Brett Brosnahan）警官。他在警队工作了六年，参加了内隐偏见课程。他与肖恩交谈过。

00:53:04.920 --> 00:53:25.670
<v Brett Brosnahan>Having a bias isn't being sexist. It's none of those things. Biased is, you know, maybe I've stopped three or four guys that look exactly like this man. And every time I've stopped them, I found guns or dope on them. So you just put the trait from the person that was guilty onto the person that's innocent.
<v Brett Brosnahan>有偏见并不等于性别歧视。它什么都不是。偏见是，你知道，也许我拦下了三四个看起来和这个人一模一样的人。而每次我拦下他们时，我都从他们身上搜出了枪或毒品。所以你就把那个有罪之人的特征加到了无辜之人的身上。

00:53:25.670 --> 00:53:31.790
<v Sean Cole>And does that, I mean, does that make you feel bad when you realize that you think that? Do you know what I mean, like about yourself?
<v Sean Cole>而且，我是说，当你意识到你有这种想法时，你会感觉糟糕吗？我的意思是，关于你自己？

00:53:31.790 --> 00:53:47.950
<v Brett Brosnahan>It is. It's scary because it's not fair. It's not fair for me to assume that this guy's going to have the same background. I mean, he might be the CEO of a company, just out for a walk, and didn't want to walk to the crosswalk. I don't know.
<v Brett Brosnahan>是的。这很可怕，因为它不公平。我假设这家伙有同样的背景是不公平的。我是说，他可能是某家公司的CEO，只是出来散散步，不想走过人行横道。我不知道。

00:53:47.950 --> 00:54:10.010
<v Sean Cole>These days, whenever Brett rolls up on a situation-- a traffic stop or questioning a suspect, whatever it is-- he says he tries to keep his mind totally open, thinking, this could be anyone. The class also made him reassess something that happened to him that's now legend at the department. It actually happened a month before he took the training, and it was horrible.
<v Sean Cole>这些天，每当布雷特遇到某种情况——交通拦截或盘问嫌疑人，不管是什么——他说他尽量让自己的思想完全开放，想着，“这可能是任何人。”这门课程也让他重新审视了一件发生在他身上，现在已成为警局传奇的事情。它实际上发生在他参加培训的前一个月，非常可怕。

00:54:10.010 --> 00:54:32.690
<v Sean Cole>Last June on a Sunday morning, two of Brett's fellow officers were shot to death in a pizza place on the north side of the city. Brett responded to the scene not long after. The other customers, of course, were freaking out, yelling, he went that way. He went that way, meaning the shooter. Brett figured it might be a revenge killing of some sort, either against cops in general or these two officers in specific.
<v Sean Cole>去年6月的一个周日上午，布雷特的两个同事在城市北边的一家披萨店被枪杀。布雷特在事发后不久赶到了现场。当然，其他顾客都吓坏了，大叫着：他往那边走了！他往那边走了！意思是枪手。布雷特心想这可能是某种报复性杀戮，要么是针对普通警察，要么是针对这两名警官。

00:54:32.690 --> 00:54:39.230
<v Brett Brosnahan>In that area, I mean, it's a high gang environment. There's a lot of gang members there. And--
<v Brett Brosnahan>在那个地区，我是说，那是个高犯罪环境。那里有很多帮派成员。而且——

00:54:39.230 --> 00:54:41.900
<v Sean Cole>Did you think, male, female, black, white, anything like that?
<v Sean Cole>你想过男性、女性、黑人、白人，类似的事情吗？

00:54:41.900 --> 00:54:53.100
<v Brett Brosnahan>Male, probably black or Hispanic. I mean, northeast area part of Las Vegas, it's a large black and Hispanic population. So, you know, you go with the odds.
<v Brett Brosnahan>男性，可能是黑人或西班牙裔。我是说，拉斯维加斯东北部地区，那里有大量黑人和西班牙裔人口。所以，你知道，你就照概率来判断。

00:54:53.100 --> 00:55:17.730
<v Sean Cole>Brett takes off on foot in the direction everyone's pointing and eventually ends up at a Walmart. Customers, employees, everyone is flooding out of the store screaming. So, now, this isn't just an isolated shooting of two cops. This is an active shooter in a crowded, public place. And at this point, the color of the suspect in Brett's mind changes from black or Hispanic to white.
<v Sean Cole>布雷特沿着每个人指的方向步行追去，最终到达了一家沃尔玛。顾客、员工，每个人都尖叫着从店里涌出。所以，现在，这不仅仅是两名警察被孤立枪击的事件。这是在拥挤、公共场所发生的活跃枪手事件。在这一点上，布雷特脑海中嫌疑人的肤色从黑人或西班牙裔变成了白人。

00:55:17.730 --> 00:55:41.840
<v Sean Cole>Brett knows about active-shooter situations. He trains other officers in how to deal with these cases. Columbine, Aurora, Sandy Hook-- when Brett thinks active shooter, he thinks young white male. The whole story is too long to tell here, but in short, Brett ultimately finds himself in an empty Walmart getting ready to ambush the gunman in the automotive section.
<v Sean Cole>布雷特了解活跃枪手的情况。他训练其他警察如何处理这些案件。科伦拜恩、奥罗拉、桑迪胡克——当布雷特想到活跃枪手时，他想到的是年轻白人男性。整个故事在这里说不完，但简而言之，布雷特最终发现自己在一个空荡荡的沃尔玛里，准备在汽车区伏击持枪歹徒。

00:55:41.840 --> 00:55:47.250
<v Sean Cole>It's a young white male, semi-automatic pistol in his hand. He doesn't see Brett.
<v Sean Cole>那是一个年轻的白人男性，手里拿着半自动手枪。他没看到布雷特。

00:55:47.250 --> 00:56:20.850
<v Brett Brosnahan>And I start to make my final turn to get a good angle on him. And I come almost face to face with a female, probably four or five feet away. And, keep in mind, all this now is happening in fractions of a second. But my first thought is, why is she in here? And then I look more at her and I'm like, she's not a victim. She's not a customer. She wants to be exactly where she is right now.
<v Brett Brosnahan>我开始做最后一次转弯以获得一个好的角度。我几乎和一个女性面对面了，大约只有四五英尺远。而且，请记住，现在这一切都发生在几分之一秒内。但我的第一个想法是：她为什么在这里？然后我更多地看着她，心想：她不是受害者。她不是顾客。她想确切地待在现在的那个位置。

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:37.060
<v Sean Cole>In all the commotion, people yelling and pointing and telling Brett where the shooter was, no one ever mentioned there were two of them. Brett and the woman exchanged gunfire from four or five feet away. Miraculously, she missed him. Brett hit her in the shoulder.
<v Sean Cole>在所有的骚乱中，人们大喊大叫、指点并告诉布雷特枪手在哪里，没有人提到有两个人。布雷特和那个女人从四五英尺外互相开枪。奇迹般地，她没打中他。布雷特击中了她的肩膀。

00:56:37.060 --> 00:56:53.390
<v Sean Cole>He ran out of the store and let another team of officers take over. After a firefight, the male shooter was dead. His wife shot herself in the head. So this was what was on Brett's mind when he went into the implicit bias training class a month later.
<v Sean Cole>他跑出店外，让另一组警察接手。经过一场交火，男枪手死了。他的妻子朝自己的头上开了一枪。所以这就是布雷特一个月后去参加内隐偏见培训班时脑子里想的事情。

00:56:53.390 --> 00:57:10.720
<v Brett Brosnahan>When they started to explain that these biases aren't based on being racist, they're not based on being sexist, they're just based on our experience, I was like, oh my gosh. I've been teaching this for years. Active shooters are white guys. Active shooters, when we role play, are white officers that act as the suspect.
<v Brett Brosnahan>当他们开始解释这些偏见不是基于种族主义、不是基于性别歧视，它们只是基于我们的经验时，我心想：噢，天哪。我已经教了这套很多年了。活跃枪手是白人。当我们进行角色扮演时，活跃枪手是扮演嫌疑人的白人警察。

00:57:10.720 --> 00:57:29.280
<v Brett Brosnahan>We never throw a female in the mix. So I had that aha moment. Wow, that's what happened to me. I thought that this woman wasn't going to be a threat to me and let her remain as she was a little bit longer than I probably should have.
<v Brett Brosnahan>我们从不让女性参与其中。所以我有了那个“啊哈”时刻。哇，这就是发生在我身上的事。我以为这个女人不会对我构成威胁，让她保持现状的时间可能比我应该做的要长。

00:57:29.280 --> 00:57:46.560
<v Sean Cole>It was like a real-life version of the computer test that researchers do in the lab. White female, armed-- shoot or don't shoot? And Brett paused for just a second.
<v Sean Cole>这就像研究人员在实验室里做的电脑测试的现实版本。白人女性，武装——开枪还是不开枪？布雷特迟疑了一秒钟。

00:57:46.560 --> 00:58:06.560
<v Robyn Semien>I asked my police officer friend in New York, the one I watched the Eric Garner video with, if she thought some sort of bias training would be valuable for the NYPD. And she literally rolled her eyes and said it was insulting to tell her she didn't know how to talk to black people. Roughly half of her coworkers at the NYPD aren't white.
<v Robyn Semien>我问我在纽约的警察朋友——那个和我一起看埃里克·加纳视频的人——她是否认为某种偏见训练对纽约警局有价值。她字面意思地翻了个白眼，说告诉她她不知道如何与黑人交谈是侮辱她。她在纽约警局的同事中，大约有一半不是白人。

00:58:06.560 --> 00:58:30.740
<v Robyn Semien>The thing she felt way more urgently was how hard the past months have been, ever since Eric Garner's death, the hardest for her in all of her years at the NYPD. She says crowds form when she makes arrests more than ever. It feels more dangerous. She feels like she's risking her life every day for a city of people who hate her. She feels alone and threatened.
<v Robyn Semien>她感受更强烈的是过去几个月过得有多艰难，自从埃里克·加纳死后，这是她在纽约警局多年来最困难的一年。她说现在当她逮捕人时，形成的人群比以往任何时候都多。感觉更危险了。她觉得她每天都在为一个憎恨她的城市的人民冒着生命危险。她感到孤独和受到威胁。

00:58:30.740 --> 00:58:36.810
<v Marla Stevens>And you know, I have to point out to our classes, too, is the public biased towards us? Sure they are.
<v Marla Stevens>你知道，我也必须指出我的班级，公众对我们有偏见吗？当然有。

00:58:36.810 --> 00:58:46.800
<v Robyn Semien>Again, Marla Stevens, one of the implicit bias instructors. She says lots of cops have this feeling my friend has, and it's actually useful in her class, something to talk about.
<v Robyn Semien>再次重申，玛拉·史蒂文斯，内隐偏见讲师之一。她说很多警察都有我朋友这种感觉，这实际上在她的课上很有用，是有话可谈的内容。

00:58:46.800 --> 00:59:12.010
<v Marla Stevens>Do they have stereotypes about us? They most certainly do. Which, you know, it's aggravating, too, so turn that around. You know, if you don't like that being thrown on you by people that don't know you, how do you think that people on the street feel when you prejudge them and you don't them either? It's the same thing. And it's like beating your head against the wall, trying to get both sides to understand this concept. You know? It really is.
<v Marla Stevens>他们对我们有刻板印象吗？他们肯定有。这，你知道，也很恼人，所以把它反过来看看。你知道，如果你不喜欢被不认识你的人这样对待，你怎么认为街上的人在你预先评判他们而你也不认识他们时会有什么感觉？这是一回事。就像对着墙撞头，试图让双方都理解这个概念。你知道吗？真的是这样。

00:59:12.010 --> 00:59:42.240
<v Robyn Semien>This week, the FBI director, James Comey, talked about this exact problem of not being able to see each other. Everything I've been hoping a police chief would say after one of these incidents, he said to police. He told them, basically, I'm one of you. My grandfather was a cop. I have great affection and respect for police officers. And, he said, there's nothing more American than the conversation we need to have now about race.
<v Robyn Semien>本周，联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米谈到了这种无法看清彼此的确切问题。我一直希望警察局长在这些事件之一后会说的所有话，他都对警察说了。他基本上告诉他们：我是你们中的一员。我的祖父是警察。我对警察有着深厚的感情和尊重。而且，他说，没有比我们现在需要进行的关于种族的谈话更符合美国精神的事了。

00:59:42.240 --> 01:00:01.500
<v James Comey>I am descended from Irish immigrants. A century ago, the Irish knew well how American society and law enforcement viewed them-- as drunks, ruffians, and criminals. The Irish had some tough times, but little compares to the experience on our soil of black Americans.
<v James Comey>我是爱尔兰移民的后裔。一个世纪前，爱尔兰人很清楚美国社会和执法部门如何看待他们——醉鬼、暴徒和罪犯。爱尔兰人经历过一些艰难时期，但很少有能与美国黑人在我们这片土地上的经历相比。

01:00:01.500 --> 01:01:04.150
<v James Comey>That experience should be part of every American's consciousness. And we, especially those of us who enjoy the privilege that comes with being the majority, must confront the biases that are an inescapable part of the human condition. We must speak the truth about our shortcomings as law enforcement and fight to get better. We simply must speak to each other honestly about all these hard truths.
<v James Comey>那种经历应该成为每个美国人意识的一部分。而我们，特别是那些享有身为多数群体所带来的特权的人，必须正视那些身为人类不可避免的偏见。我们必须诚实地谈论我们作为执法部门的缺点，并努力去变得更好。我们仅仅需要诚实地就所有这些艰苦的真相互相交流。

01:01:04.150 --> 01:01:14.630
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Sean Cole with Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Brian Reed, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Editing by Joel Lovell.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的节目今天由肖恩·科尔制作，斯蒂芬妮·富（Stephanie Foo）、查纳·乔菲-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）、米基·米克、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar）、布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）、罗宾·塞米恩、阿丽莎·希普（Alissa Shipp）和南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）共同参与。我们的高级制作人是朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）。编辑是乔尔·洛维尔（Joel Lovell）。

01:01:14.630 --> 01:01:30.060
<v Ira Glass>Production help from Simon Adler. Seth Lind is our operations director. Emily Condon is our production manager. Elise Bergerson is our office manager. Adrianne Mathiowetz runs our website. Elna Baker scouts stories for our show. Research help from Michelle Harris and Christopher Swetala. Music help from Damien Graef and Rob Geddis.
<v Ira Glass>制作帮助来自西蒙·阿德勒（Simon Adler）。塞思·林德（Seth Lind）是我们的运营总监。艾米丽·康顿（Emily Condon）是我们的制作经理。埃莉斯·伯格森（Elise Bergerson）是我们的办公室经理。阿德里安娜·马蒂奥维茨（Adrianne Mathiowetz）负责我们的网站。埃尔娜·贝克（Elna Baker）为我们的节目发掘故事。研究帮助来自米歇尔·哈里斯（Michelle Harris）和克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala）。音乐帮助来自达米安·格拉夫（Damien Graef）和罗伯·格迪斯（Rob Geddis）。

01:01:30.060 --> 01:01:44.020
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

01:01:44.020 --> 01:01:53.770
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks as always to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. He wants you to know, he's down.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由PRX（公共广播交易所）传送到公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生。他想让你知道，他是支持的。

01:01:53.770 --> 01:01:59.330
<v Man>Oh, you know, I like black people. I like black men. I don't have anything against them.
<v Man>噢，你知道，我喜欢黑人。我喜欢黑人男性。我没有任何针对他们的。

01:01:59.330 --> 01:02:04.330
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周回来，带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
